WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.168 --> 00:00:02.460
NICK ORLOVE: Hey, we're late
for the small data meetup.

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:03.205
Are you coming?

00:00:03.205 --> 00:00:03.770
STEPHANIE WONG: I can't.

00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:05.603
I'm waiting for this
BigQuery job to finish.

00:00:05.603 --> 00:00:06.895
Can you wait 18.2 seconds?

00:00:06.895 --> 00:00:08.510
NICK ORLOVE: But then
all the sparkling water's

00:00:08.510 --> 00:00:09.218
going to be gone.

00:00:09.218 --> 00:00:09.950
Come on.

00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:11.060
STEPHANIE WONG:
Well, at that point,

00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:12.200
is it even worth going then?

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:14.300
NICK ORLOVE: Well, I bet if
you use BigQuery's clustering

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:15.980
and partitioning
features, your queries

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:17.437
would run much
faster and cheaper.

00:00:17.437 --> 00:00:18.770
STEPHANIE WONG: That'd be great.

00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:19.640
Can you show me how?

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:20.932
NICK ORLOVE: Yeah, let's do it.

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:30.830
See, BigQuery's a great tool for
analyzing huge amounts of data

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:32.990
quickly, but there
are plenty of times

00:00:32.990 --> 00:00:35.430
where you only care to
analyze a subset of your data.

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:37.040
For example, you may
only want to look

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:39.350
at data over a
particular period of time

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:42.350
or over a particular column.

00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:44.510
If you check out the
illustration on the screen,

00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:46.670
you can see that I'm
converting my unordered table

00:00:46.670 --> 00:00:50.180
on the left into a date
partition table on the right

00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:53.100
and clustering my
tags closer together.

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:55.190
This is really going
to optimize my queries

00:00:55.190 --> 00:00:57.540
when I analyze my data.

00:00:57.540 --> 00:00:59.790
Now looking at my
GCB project, you

00:00:59.790 --> 00:01:01.690
can see that I have
a Stack Overflow data

00:01:01.690 --> 00:01:05.580
set, which contains a table
with 10.8 million Stack Overflow

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:09.720
questions which have
been gathered since 2014.

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:11.980
This was pulled from the
BigQuery Stack Overflow

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:16.430
public data set by the way,
which everyone has access to.

00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:19.310
From the schema, you can
see that this table contains

00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:22.130
a bunch of great information
from each Stack Overflow

00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:27.560
question such as the ID,
title, body, creation date,

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:29.810
number of comments, what
it's been tagged with,

00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:31.160
view count, et cetera.

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:34.610
That's a lot of data across
10.8 million Stack Overflow

00:01:34.610 --> 00:01:36.220
questions.

00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:38.220
So let's say you want to
gather some information

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:40.710
on the types of questions
being asked about Android

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:42.090
in the last six months.

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:45.610
To accomplish this, I wrote
this simple SQL query.

00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:48.030
We can see that we only care
about dates in the last six

00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:51.630
months and anything that
has a tag Android in it.

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:54.660
When running this query
against my standard table,

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:57.330
it has to scan all
of the data and all

00:01:57.330 --> 00:02:00.520
of the tags and the
creation date column,

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:02.550
so you can see that
it takes a few seconds

00:02:02.550 --> 00:02:06.020
and scans the full
18 gigabytes of data.

00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:09.100
Now if we set up the
table to be partitioned,

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:11.650
BigQuery will only scan
the parts of the table that

00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:15.050
fall into this date range.

00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:17.750
Ideally, you should start using
partitioning when you first

00:02:17.750 --> 00:02:18.930
create your table.

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:22.250
However, I'll run a DDL, or
Data Definition Language,

00:02:22.250 --> 00:02:25.820
query to create a new table
from this existing table

00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:29.120
and use the creation underscore
date column as my custom

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:30.020
partitioning column.

00:02:33.333 --> 00:02:35.500
Now let's run that query
against my partition table.

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:44.800
You can see that it returns
in about a quarter of the time

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:47.180
and scans about a tenth
of the amount of data.

00:02:47.180 --> 00:02:48.237
Impressive.

00:02:48.237 --> 00:02:50.070
STEPHANIE WONG: So let
me get this straight.

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:52.110
BigQuery is taking care
of all of the hard work?

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:53.160
NICK ORLOVE: Yep.

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:56.040
You just tell it what date
field you want to partition by,

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:58.230
and BigQuery knows exactly
what partition ranges

00:02:58.230 --> 00:02:59.850
to use to maximize efficiency.

00:02:59.850 --> 00:03:01.267
STEPHANIE WONG:
But what if I want

00:03:01.267 --> 00:03:03.510
to partition my data on
something like a text column?

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:05.427
NICK ORLOVE: That's where
clustering comes in.

00:03:05.427 --> 00:03:07.470
Let me show you.

00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:08.940
Looking at this
query, we're taking

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:10.800
advantage of partitioning
because we're

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:12.690
filtering by creation date.

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:16.140
However, the query still needs
a scan across all the tags

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:18.900
to find out which ones are
Android, even though we're only

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:22.020
looking for a single keyword.

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:24.270
Clustering is a way for
BigQuery to optimize

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:26.280
the placement of
your data and allows

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:29.130
it to store alike
data closer together

00:03:29.130 --> 00:03:31.080
to make queries more efficient.

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:34.830
Typically clustering works best
on high cardinality fields,

00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:37.620
which maximizes the amount
of benefit you'll get.

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:40.480
By the way, you can
cluster on multiple columns

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:43.680
so you aren't
limited to just one.

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:45.600
Just like partitioning,
you should set up

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:47.680
clustering at the get go.

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:51.930
So I'll run a similar DDL as
before to create a new table,

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:54.870
but this time we'll cluster
our data using the column tags.

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:06.010
So now if we run these
queries with clustering,

00:04:06.010 --> 00:04:08.670
we can see that we get our
results slightly faster,

00:04:08.670 --> 00:04:12.350
and we're now scanning megabytes
of data instead of gigabytes.

00:04:12.350 --> 00:04:14.550
STEPHANIE WONG: So if I
use BigQuery's partitioning

00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:16.350
and clustering, I'll
only scan the data

00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:18.420
that I need to save
both time and money.

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:19.500
NICK ORLOVE: Exactly.

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:21.329
It takes a little bit
more work upfront,

00:04:21.329 --> 00:04:22.760
but it's definitely worth it.

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:24.520
STEPHANIE WONG: And for those
of you watching at home,

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:25.770
check out the link
in the description

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:27.180
to try it out for yourselves.

00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:29.820
Don't forget to like, comment,
and subscribe for more great

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:32.850
Google Cloud Platform content.

00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:35.900
